course: dictionary
lesson: 1646
title: One word, many meanings: chime
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A chime is a clear ringing sound, like a bell or a clock. Chimes can also be a set of bells or metal tubes that ring musically. The context tells you which meaning is right. Read the sentence and ask: what is ringing, or what is being said?
explain: «Mrs Platt, who moans about bikes and wind chimes, is first at the door when Sam's dad breaks his leg on the ice, with a casserole and a shovel for the drive.» Here wind chimes is a plural noun, so it means a set of bells or metal tubes that hang outside and ring when the wind blows.
explain: Chime also works as a verb, so you can say the clock chimed three. Watch for the phrases too. To chime in means to join a conversation suddenly. To chime with means to agree or fit with something, and it is a more formal use.
fact: A chime is a clear ringing sound made by a bell or a clock.
fact: Chimes can mean a set of bells or metal tubes that ring musically, such as wind chimes.
fact: As a verb, chime means to ring with a clear sound: the clock chimed three.
fact: Chime in means to join a conversation suddenly.
fact: Chime with means to agree or fit with something, and it is a formal phrase.
exercise: «The clock chimed three as we walked in.» What does chimed mean here? | *Rang with a clear musical sound | Ran very fast | Broke into pieces | Went quiet and still
exercise: Asha wanted to chime in on the chat. What does chime in mean? | To leave the room quietly | *To join a conversation suddenly | To sleep in late | To sing a long song
exercise: «Wind chimes hung on the porch and swayed in the breeze.» What are wind chimes? | *A set of bells or metal tubes that ring musically | A type of bird | A kind of kite | A garden hose
test: «The church clock gave a chime at midnight.» Which meaning fits this sentence? | A dark colour | A type of cake | *A clear ringing sound made by a bell | A long walk
test: Her story did not chime with what we saw. What does chime with mean? | To run away | To clap along | To ring loudly | *To fit or agree with
exam: A clock chimes once for each hour, and it has chimed five times since the start of the day. What time is it? | Four o'clock | *Five o'clock | Three o'clock | Six o'clock
